//! English explanations of the DNS Statistics window (G4.1h), scope `dns_statistics`, sorted by
//! key.

/// Explanations.
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("dns_statistics.answered", "## Answered by\nWho answered each query: the cache, an upstream, a stale answer (RFC 8767), a static entry, the adlist or a zone, or refused (outside Allowed From, over the per-client or the concurrent limit, malformed)."),
    ("dns_statistics.answers_per_sec", "## Answers/s\nThis server's answers per second over the last minute."),
    ("dns_statistics.by_cache", "## Cache\nAnswered from the cache, nobody asked."),
    ("dns_statistics.by_refused", "## Refused / Limit\nNot really answered: client outside Allowed From (REFUSED), over `max-queries-per-client` (dropped over UDP, REFUSED over TCP), over `max-concurrent-queries` (SERVFAIL) or malformed."),
    ("dns_statistics.by_stale", "## Stale\nNo upstream answered and the cache had an expired answer still inside the serve-stale window: it went out with TTL 30 s (RFC 8767)."),
    ("dns_statistics.by_static", "## Static / Adlist\nAnswered by the router itself: static entries (`/ip dns static`), adlist blocks, authoritative zones and special names (RFC 6761)."),
    ("dns_statistics.by_upstream", "## Upstream\nAsked of an upstream server (its answer went into the cache)."),
    ("dns_statistics.cache_entries", "## Entries\nAnswers held now (positive and negative, any type)."),
    ("dns_statistics.cache_hits", "## Cache hits\nOf the queries that needed the resolver (cache, upstream or stale), the share the cache answered, averaged over the period (not since the reset).\n\nUnder ~50 % on a usual LAN, `cache-size` may be small (see Evictions in the Cache tab) or many names are unique."),
    ("dns_statistics.cache_used", "## Used / Max\nThe entries' estimated memory against `cache-size` (IP › DNS)."),
    ("dns_statistics.client", "## Client\nThe address that asked."),
    ("dns_statistics.client_queries", "## Queries\nThis client's queries since the reset."),
    ("dns_statistics.client_refused", "## Refused\nThis client's queries refused (outside Allowed From) or dropped by the per-client limit."),
    ("dns_statistics.client_share", "## %\nThe share of all queries that came from this client."),
    ("dns_statistics.clients", "## Top clients\nThe addresses that asked most since the reset. Memory is fixed: with more clients than fit, the one with the lowest count makes room (Space-Saving); \"≤\" means part of the count may belong to the ones it replaced."),
    ("dns_statistics.encrypted_in", "## DoT / DoH in\nThe router does not serve DNS over TLS or DNS over HTTPS to the LAN yet: it only uses them to ask upstreams (IP › DNS, `use-doh-server`)."),
    ("dns_statistics.evicted", "## Evictions\nEntries still valid thrown away to fit a new one, since the reset. Always growing: `cache-size` is small for the LAN, or a unique-names attack pushes the hot names out (see G4.1x)."),
    ("dns_statistics.expired", "## Expired\nEntries removed after their TTL and the serve-stale window."),
    ("dns_statistics.in_flight", "## In flight\nQueries waiting for an upstream now, against `max-concurrent-queries`. At the limit the next query gets SERVFAIL at once (the client tries its other server). Under it, the most at once since the reset and how often the limit was hit."),
    ("dns_statistics.latency", "## Latency p50 / p99\nThe time from reading the query to sending the reply, over the last minute: the median (p50, half are faster) and the 99th percentile (only 1 % are slower). With no readings in the minute, since the reset.\n\nThe cache answers in microseconds; a high p99 shows slow upstreams or timeouts."),
    ("dns_statistics.malformed", "## Malformed\nMessages refused before the chain: shorter than a header, unreadable (FORMERR), another opcode (NOTIMP), another class or a misplaced AXFR. Many of them mean a scan or a broken client."),
    ("dns_statistics.period", "## Period\nThe window of the graph and of the cards that read it (queries' peak, cache hits, SERVFAIL): the last hour (1 s points), 24 h (1 min) or 7 days (5 min). The history is Graphing's (System › Graphing), series `dns`."),
    ("dns_statistics.queries_per_sec", "## Queries per second\nThe large number is the average of the last 10 s. Under it, the period's highest value and when (UTC).\n\nAn unusual peak with no new client may be a random-subdomain attack or a device in a loop."),
    ("dns_statistics.rcodes", "## Response codes\nThe RCODE of each reply (RFC 1035 §4.1.1): NOERROR, NXDOMAIN (no such name), SERVFAIL, REFUSED, FORMERR (malformed query), NOTIMP (operation not supported). Dropped: no reply (the limit over UDP, or junk)."),
    ("dns_statistics.reset", "## Reset Statistics\nSets the counters back to zero (`ip.dns.statistics.reset`). The config and the cache stay and nothing is rolled back: it is counting only. The graph's history stays."),
    ("dns_statistics.rtt_histogram", "## Round trips by range\nHow many answers fell in each range: under 1 ms, 1–10, 10–100, 100–500, 500–1600 and over 1600 ms, since the reset. The ends always show: the range over 1600 ms is the near-timeouts. With a row selected in the table, that server only."),
    ("dns_statistics.rtt_p50", "## p50 ms\nThe median round trip of its answers, ms, over the last minute (since the reset when it had none). Computed from the histogram's buckets: a bucket's width is the error."),
    ("dns_statistics.rtt_p95", "## p95 ms\nThe 95th percentile of the round trip: only 5 % took longer. Over 500 ms the server is `slow`."),
    ("dns_statistics.server", "## Server\nThe upstream server's address."),
    ("dns_statistics.servfail", "## SERVFAIL\nThe share of replies that were SERVFAIL, averaged over the period. Under it, the most common extended error (EDE, RFC 8914): 22 \"No Reachable Authority\" is no upstream answered; 13 \"Cached Error\" is a recent failure still held (RFC 9520); 18 \"Prohibited\" is a client outside Allowed From. The codes are counted, not sent in the reply yet."),
    ("dns_statistics.socket_drops", "## Socket drops\nQueries the kernel threw away because the UDP socket's queue was full before a thread read them (`/proc/net/udp`, the drops column). Any number here is a client left without an answer: the router could not keep up with the burst."),
    ("dns_statistics.stale_served", "## Stale served\nExpired answers given because no upstream answered (RFC 8767): the client was not left without an answer during the outage."),
    ("dns_statistics.state", "## State\n`up` answers well; `slow` with p95 over 500 ms or many timeouts in the last minute; `down` failed and goes last for a minute. The time is since the last change between up and down."),
    ("dns_statistics.tab_cache", "## Cache\nThe answer cache the router and the LAN share: entries, bytes used against `cache-size`, evictions, expiries, stale answers served (RFC 8767) and the types cached and asked."),
    ("dns_statistics.tab_overview", "## Overview\nThe LAN DNS forwarder at a glance: the cards that matter now, the period's graph, who answered, the response codes, the transport, the warnings and the top clients."),
    ("dns_statistics.tab_upstreams", "## Upstreams\nThe upstream servers the resolver asks: how each is asked (UDP, DoT, DoH), answers per second, round-trip time, timeouts, SERVFAIL, truncated answers and the state (up, slow, down). Below, the round trips by range."),
    ("dns_statistics.tc_tcp", "## Truncated\nIts UDP answers that came truncated and were asked again over TCP (RFC 7766 §5)."),
    ("dns_statistics.tcp4", "## TCP IPv4\nQueries over TCP from IPv4 clients (large answers, DNSSEC, or after a TC)."),
    ("dns_statistics.tcp6", "## TCP IPv6\nQueries over TCP from IPv6 clients."),
    ("dns_statistics.timeouts", "## Timeout %\nOf the times it was asked since the reset, how many got no answer within `query-server-timeout`. Each timeout holds a concurrent-query slot and delays the client."),
    ("dns_statistics.transport", "## Transport\nHow the queries came: UDP or TCP, IPv4 or IPv6; the truncated replies (TC) and the kernel's drops on the UDP socket."),
    ("dns_statistics.truncated", "## Truncated (TC)\nReplies too large for the client's UDP size: sent without records and with TC set, and the client asks again over TCP (RFC 1035 §4.2.1, RFC 6891)."),
    ("dns_statistics.types_asked", "## Asked by type\nThe queries since the reset by the record type asked."),
    ("dns_statistics.types_cached", "## Cached by type\nThe cache's entries by record type (A, AAAA, HTTPS…)."),
    ("dns_statistics.udp4", "## UDP IPv4\nQueries over UDP from IPv4 clients."),
    ("dns_statistics.udp6", "## UDP IPv6\nQueries over UDP from IPv6 clients."),
    ("dns_statistics.upstream_servfail", "## SERVFAIL %\nOf the queries to it, how many came back SERVFAIL (the next goes to another server)."),
    ("dns_statistics.via", "## Via\nHow it was asked last: UDP (TCP when the answer is truncated), DOT (DNS over TLS, RFC 7858) or DOH (DNS over HTTPS, RFC 8484)."),
    ("dns_statistics.warnings", "## Warnings\nWhat the router noticed in the last minute: an upstream down or timing out, SERVFAIL high, the concurrent limit reached, the cache evicting valid answers, socket drops. The router decides; the window only shows them."),
];
